# Privacy and record access

## Service access

- Authorized service personnel may access consultation records when necessary to provide the service.
- Operational access does not make records available to every person or organization.

## Employer information

- Employers do not receive individual consultation content.
- Employers may receive aggregated utilization reports.
- Aggregated reports do not include individual medical conversations.

## Profile correction

- A user may ask support to correct basic profile information.
- A profile request does not authorize changes to prescriptions or clinical decisions.

These rules belong to the fictional demonstration service. This document does not claim a real regulatory certification and contains no real patient, employee, or consultation records.

## Separation between service operations and employers

Access by authorized service personnel is limited to what is necessary for service delivery. This rule does not permit unrestricted access. It also does not authorize the assistant to expose records, summarize an individual consultation for a third party, or reveal content at an employer's request.

An aggregated report describes collective use. It does not include the text of individual medical conversations. A claim that an employer receives every conversation must therefore be corrected by distinguishing aggregate reporting from individual content.

## Profile corrections

Support can receive requests to correct basic profile data. This process does not extend to prescriptions and does not permit support staff to change clinical decisions.

The knowledge base explains the channel but cannot confirm that a specific correction was completed. Request state belongs to operational systems.

## Safe use of information

Conversation history helps the assistant follow references, but it is not evidence about clinical records. A user's statement about what an employer saw also cannot replace the approved policy.

If a question requires a detail absent from this document, the assistant must identify the evidence gap. It must not import an external rule or claim a certification.

## Examples

For “Can my employer read my consultation?”, the knowledge base supports a negative answer about individual content and can explain the existence of aggregated reports.

For “Who can access a record to provide the service?”, access is limited to authorized personnel when necessary.

For “How do I correct my profile name?”, the knowledge base directs the user to support. For “Can support correct my prescription?”, the prescription policy prohibits that action.

## Dataset nature

All service names, contacts, and rules are fictional. Nothing in this demonstration should be interpreted as the policy of a real organization or advice about a real clinical case.
